  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Strix",
          "informationUri": "https://strix.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-862",
              "name": "Unauthenticated Stripe Verification in apply_deposit RPC Allows Unlimited Wallet Balance Inflation",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unauthenticated Stripe Verification in apply_deposit RPC Allows Unlimited Wallet Balance Inflation"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The `apply_deposit` PostgreSQL function is granted `EXECUTE` to the `authenticated` role, allowing any authenticated user to call it directly via the Supabase JS client and credit any arbitrary dollar amount to their own (or another user's) wallet without making any real Stripe payment. The function accepts a caller-controlled `p_amount` and a `p_payment_intent_id` string that is never verified against Stripe's API, making it trivially exploitable for unlimited money creation."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The `apply_deposit` PostgreSQL function is granted `EXECUTE` to the `authenticated` role, allowing any authenticated user to call it directly via the Supabase JS client and credit any arbitrary dollar amount to their own (or another user's) wallet without making any real Stripe payment. The function accepts a caller-controlled `p_amount` and a `p_payment_intent_id` string that is never verified against Stripe's API, making it trivially exploitable for unlimited money creation.\n\nAny authenticated user can credit their wallet with an arbitrary dollar amount (e.g., $999,999) without making any actual payment. They can also supply any other user's UUID as `p_user_id` to credit a victim's balance. This breaks the financial integrity of the entire platform: attackers can withdraw real funds (via bounty completion, Stripe Connect payouts, etc.) that were never deposited, resulting in direct financial loss for the platform operator. The attack is silent — there is no Stripe charge, webhook, or admin alert triggered.\n\n1. **Revoke the `authenticated` grant on `apply_deposit`** immediately: `REVOKE EXECUTE ON FUNCTION apply_deposit(UUID, NUMERIC, TEXT, JSONB) FROM authenticated;`. The function should only be callable by `service_role` (backend Edge Functions and webhooks) — never directly by client-side authenticated users.\n\n2. **Add a caller-identity guard inside the function**: At the start of the function body, add `IF auth.role() = 'authenticated' AND auth.uid() <> p_user_id THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'Caller may not deposit to another user'; END IF;`. This is a defense-in-depth measure; the grant revocation above is the primary fix.\n\n3. **Verify the payment intent server-side**: The `/wallet/deposit` Edge Function should call `stripe.paymentIntents.retrieve(paymentIntentId)` and assert `pi.status === 'succeeded'` and `pi.metadata.user_id === userId` before calling `apply_deposit`. It should use `pi.amount / 100` as the canonical amount instead of the client-supplied value. This eliminates the amount-manipulation attack that remains even if direct RPC access is removed (see companion finding).\n\n4. **Add a `pi_` prefix length/format check** on `p_payment_intent_id` inside the RPC as a secondary guard against fabricated strings, though the grant revocation is the definitive fix.",
                "markdown": "The `apply_deposit` PostgreSQL function is granted `EXECUTE` to the `authenticated` role, allowing any authenticated user to call it directly via the Supabase JS client and credit any arbitrary dollar amount to their own (or another user's) wallet without making any real Stripe payment. The function accepts a caller-controlled `p_amount` and a `p_payment_intent_id` string that is never verified against Stripe's API, making it trivially exploitable for unlimited money creation.\n\nAny authenticated user can credit their wallet with an arbitrary dollar amount (e.g., $999,999) without making any actual payment. They can also supply any other user's UUID as `p_user_id` to credit a victim's balance. This breaks the financial integrity of the entire platform: attackers can withdraw real funds (via bounty completion, Stripe Connect payouts, etc.) that were never deposited, resulting in direct financial loss for the platform operator. The attack is silent — there is no Stripe charge, webhook, or admin alert triggered.\n\n1. **Revoke the `authenticated` grant on `apply_deposit`** immediately: `REVOKE EXECUTE ON FUNCTION apply_deposit(UUID, NUMERIC, TEXT, JSONB) FROM authenticated;`. The function should only be callable by `service_role` (backend Edge Functions and webhooks) — never directly by client-side authenticated users.\n\n2. **Add a caller-identity guard inside the function**: At the start of the function body, add `IF auth.role() = 'authenticated' AND auth.uid() <> p_user_id THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'Caller may not deposit to another user'; END IF;`. This is a defense-in-depth measure; the grant revocation above is the primary fix.\n\n3. **Verify the payment intent server-side**: The `/wallet/deposit` Edge Function should call `stripe.paymentIntents.retrieve(paymentIntentId)` and assert `pi.status === 'succeeded'` and `pi.metadata.user_id === userId` before calling `apply_deposit`. It should use `pi.amount / 100` as the canonical amount instead of the client-supplied value. This eliminates the amount-manipulation attack that remains even if direct RPC access is removed (see companion finding).\n\n4. **Add a `pi_` prefix length/format check** on `p_payment_intent_id` inside the RPC as a secondary guard against fabricated strings, though the grant revocation is the definitive fix."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "6.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-862",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html"
            }
          ],
          "version": "1.5.3"
        }
      },
      "results": [
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-862",
          "ruleIndex": 0,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Unauthenticated Stripe Verification in apply_deposit RPC Allows Unlimited Wallet Balance Inflation\n\nThe `apply_deposit` PostgreSQL function is granted `EXECUTE` to the `authenticated` role, allowing any authenticated user to call it directly via the Supabase JS client and credit any arbitrary dollar amount to their own (or another user's) wallet without making any real Stripe payment. The function accepts a caller-controlled `p_amount` and a `p_payment_intent_id` string that is never verified against Stripe's API, making it trivially exploitable for unlimited money creation."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
