                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "GRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION apply_deposit(UUID, NUMERIC, TEXT, JSONB) TO authenticated;\nGRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION apply_deposit(UUID, NUMERIC, TEXT, JSONB) TO service_role;"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Revoke authenticated EXECUTE grant — primary fix"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "supabase/migrations/20260310_apply_deposit.sql"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 13,
                  "endLine": 16,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "DECLARE\n  v_tx_id UUID;\nBEGIN\n  -- Try to insert the wallet transaction."
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Add caller-identity guard inside the function body"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "/rpc/apply_deposit",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "supabase/migrations/20260310_apply_deposit.sql"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 45,
                        "endLine": 46
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "-- Direct authenticated-user access removed: apply_deposit is backend-only.\n-- All legitimate callers use service_role (Edge Functions, webhooks).\nGRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION apply_deposit(UUID, NUMERIC, TEXT, JSONB) TO service_role;"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "supabase/migrations/20260310_apply_deposit.sql"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 13,
                        "endLine": 16
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "DECLARE\n  v_tx_id UUID;\nBEGIN\n  -- Defense-in-depth: authenticated callers may only deposit to themselves.\n  IF auth.role() = 'authenticated' AND auth.uid() <> p_user_id THEN\n    RAISE EXCEPTION 'apply_deposit: caller may not deposit to a different user'\n      USING ERRCODE = '42501';\n  END IF;\n  -- Try to insert the wallet transaction."
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "1. **Revoke the `authenticated` grant on `apply_deposit`** immediately: `REVOKE EXECUTE ON FUNCTION apply_deposit(UUID, NUMERIC, TEXT, JSONB) FROM authenticated;`. The function should only be callable by `service_role` (backend Edge Functions and webhooks) — never directly by client-side authenticated users.\n\n2. **Add a caller-identity guard inside the function**: At the start of the function body, add `IF auth.role() = 'authenticated' AND auth.uid() <> p_user_id THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'Caller may not deposit to another user'; END IF;`. This is a defense-in-depth measure; the grant revocation above is the primary fix.\n\n3. **Verify the payment intent server-side**: The `/wallet/deposit` Edge Function should call `stripe.paymentIntents.retrieve(paymentIntentId)` and assert `pi.status === 'succeeded'` and `pi.metadata.user_id === userId` before calling `apply_deposit`. It should use `pi.amount / 100` as the canonical amount instead of the client-supplied value. This eliminates the amount-manipulation attack that remains even if direct RPC access is removed (see companion finding).\n\n4. **Add a `pi_` prefix length/format check** on `p_payment_intent_id` inside the RPC as a secondary guard against fabricated strings, though the grant revocation is the definitive fix.",
                "markdown": "1. **Revoke the `authenticated` grant on `apply_deposit`** immediately: `REVOKE EXECUTE ON FUNCTION apply_deposit(UUID, NUMERIC, TEXT, JSONB) FROM authenticated;`. The function should only be callable by `service_role` (backend Edge Functions and webhooks) — never directly by client-side authenticated users.\n\n2. **Add a caller-identity guard inside the function**: At the start of the function body, add `IF auth.role() = 'authenticated' AND auth.uid() <> p_user_id THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'Caller may not deposit to another user'; END IF;`. This is a defense-in-depth measure; the grant revocation above is the primary fix.\n\n3. **Verify the payment intent server-side**: The `/wallet/deposit` Edge Function should call `stripe.paymentIntents.retrieve(paymentIntentId)` and assert `pi.status === 'succeeded'` and `pi.metadata.user_id === userId` before calling `apply_deposit`. It should use `pi.amount / 100` as the canonical amount instead of the client-supplied value. This eliminates the amount-manipulation attack that remains even if direct RPC access is removed (see companion finding).\n\n4. **Add a `pi_` prefix length/format check** on `p_payment_intent_id` inside the RPC as a secondary guard against fabricated strings, though the grant revocation is the definitive fix."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "4399202b1b70bb9a47308e749114569845f845389fe7566fa88c6b8a68d6c6f1"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "6.5",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "efcbfd2a9b4963aa3154125eec939ae43ef06236556e7bcc8b4a92f8110501d1",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0001",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 6.5,
              "timestamp": "2026-08-25 00:29:29 UTC",
              "target": "/workspace/Bounty-production",
              "endpoint": "/rpc/apply_deposit",
              "method": "POST",
              "cwe": "CWE-862",
              "impact": "Any authenticated user can credit their wallet with an arbitrary dollar amount (e.g., $999,999) without making any actual payment. They can also supply any other user's UUID as `p_user_id` to credit a victim's balance. This breaks the financial integrity of the entire platform: attackers can withdraw real funds (via bounty completion, Stripe Connect payouts, etc.) that were never deposited, resulting in direct financial loss for the platform operator. The attack is silent — there is no Stripe charge, webhook, or admin alert triggered.",
              "technical_analysis": "The `apply_deposit` function defined in `supabase/migrations/20260310_apply_deposit.sql` performs two operations atomically:\n\n1. Inserts a `wallet_transactions` row with type=`deposit`, status=`completed`, and the caller-supplied `p_amount`.\n2. Updates `profiles.balance` by adding `p_amount`.\n\nThe ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING clause on `stripe_payment_intent_id` is the idempotency guard — the first call with a given `p_payment_intent_id` string wins, and subsequent calls are no-ops.\n\n**Root cause:** The function is declared `SECURITY DEFINER` (executes as the function owner, bypassing RLS) and is granted `EXECUTE` to the `authenticated` role. Inside the function there is no call to Stripe to verify the payment intent's actual charged amount, no check that the intent has status `succeeded`, and no validation that `p_user_id = auth.uid()`. Any authenticated user can call it directly from the Supabase JS client:\n\n```typescript\nconst { data } = await supabase.rpc('apply_deposit', {\n  p_user_id: targetUserId,\n  p_amount: 999999,\n  p_payment_intent_id: `pi_fake_${Date.now()}`,\n  p_metadata: {}\n});\n```\n\nThe `p_payment_intent_id` only needs to be a unique string — it is never validated against the Stripe API. Because the Edge Functions (wallet, payments, webhooks) always use the service role, they cannot be distinguished from a direct client call inside the RPC. The only signal the function uses to decide whether to apply a credit is whether that `stripe_payment_intent_id` string already exists in `wallet_transactions`.",
              "remediation_steps": "1. **Revoke the `authenticated` grant on `apply_deposit`** immediately: `REVOKE EXECUTE ON FUNCTION apply_deposit(UUID, NUMERIC, TEXT, JSONB) FROM authenticated;`. The function should only be callable by `service_role` (backend Edge Functions and webhooks) — never directly by client-side authenticated users.\n\n2. **Add a caller-identity guard inside the function**: At the start of the function body, add `IF auth.role() = 'authenticated' AND auth.uid() <> p_user_id THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'Caller may not deposit to another user'; END IF;`. This is a defense-in-depth measure; the grant revocation above is the primary fix.\n\n3. **Verify the payment intent server-side**: The `/wallet/deposit` Edge Function should call `stripe.paymentIntents.retrieve(paymentIntentId)` and assert `pi.status === 'succeeded'` and `pi.metadata.user_id === userId` before calling `apply_deposit`. It should use `pi.amount / 100` as the canonical amount instead of the client-supplied value. This eliminates the amount-manipulation attack that remains even if direct RPC access is removed (see companion finding).\n\n4. **Add a `pi_` prefix length/format check** on `p_payment_intent_id` inside the RPC as a secondary guard against fabricated strings, though the grant revocation is the definitive fix.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Sign in to the application to obtain a valid Supabase JWT for any user account.\n2. Note the authenticated user's `userId` (UUID).\n3. Using the Supabase JS client (or any HTTP client), call the `apply_deposit` RPC with a fabricated `p_payment_intent_id` (any unique string), a large `p_amount`, and `p_user_id` set to the attacker's own UUID.\n4. Observe that the wallet balance is increased by the specified amount immediately.\n5. Repeat with a different unique `p_payment_intent_id` to accumulate unlimited balance.\n6. Use the inflated balance to post/fund high-value bounties, have a confederate hunter \"complete\" them, and withdraw real platform funds via Stripe Connect payout.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
